/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A wave of field strength E (V/m) arriving at an antenna pushes charge along the metal.
   - Voc = E leff cos ψ.
   - Seen from the receiver, the antenna is a source Voc in series with its impedance ZA = Rr + RL + jXA, the same impedance it shows as a transmitter.
   - PL = |Voc|² / (8(Rr + RL)). the available power in W, with Voc a peak value
   - Under that match, for a lossless antenna, an equal power is re-radiated (scattered) through Rr: half of what the antenna collects reaches the load.
   - Effective length is the length of a uniform current element that would give the same Voc.
   - The link between effective area and directivity can be checked on the Hertzian dipole, using only results of this unit:
   - Since D = 1.5 for this dipole, Aem = D λ²/4π.
   - Take two antennas, 1 and 2, in any linear medium.
   - Received powers are not automatically equal in the two directions.
*/
