/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 6 GHz point-to-point microwave hop is 40 km long. Each end has a 1.2 m dish with aperture efficiency 0.55 and 2 dB of feeder loss; the transmitter gives 1 W and the receiver needs −75 dBm. Find the dish gain, the FSPL, the received power and the margin, and check that Friis applies.
   1. λ = 3 × 10⁸ / (6 × 10⁹) = 0.05 m.
   2. Dish gain: G = eap(πD/λ)² = 0.55 × (π × 1.2/0.05)² = 0.55 × 5685 = 3127, that is 34.95 dBi.
   3. FSPL = 32.44 + 20 log₁₀ 6000 + 20 log₁₀ 40 = 32.44 + 75.56 + 32.04 = 140.04 dB.
   4. Pt = 1 W = 30 dBm; EIRP = 30 − 2 + 34.95 = 62.95 dBm.
   5. Pr = 62.95 − 140.04 + 34.95 − 2 = −44.14 dBm.
   6. M = −44.14 − (−75) = 30.86 dB, about 31 dB of fade margin.
   7. Far field: 2D²/λ = 2 × 1.44/0.05 = 57.6 m, far less than 40 km, so the far-field formula is valid.
   Answer: G = 34.95 dBi per dish; FSPL = 140.0 dB; Pr = −44.1 dBm; margin 30.9 dB. */
